#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Drives a server with the real Bazel 9.3.2 client (the pinned oracle's C++ client).

Usage:
  scripts/server/interop-server help
  scripts/server/interop-server list
  scripts/server/interop-server selftest
  scripts/server/interop-server run [CASE...] [--probe PATH] [--bazel PATH] [--scratch DIR]
  scripts/server/interop-server run --server-binary PATH [--control] [--bazel PATH]
  scripts/server/interop-server clean [--scratch DIR]

The oracle's C++ client starts whatever is at `<server_javabase>/bin/java`, so the tool makes a
directory whose bin/java is a symlink to a copy of the server under test and runs
  bazel --output_user_root=<root> --nohome_rc --nosystem_rc --server_javabase=<dir> CMD
in a scratch workspace. The client extracts its own install base, writes server/cmdline, runs
daemonize, waits for server_info.rawproto, verifies the pid, pings, sends the RunRequest,
streams, cancels on SIGINT and waits for the process on shutdown. --bazel must be the pinned
oracle (its sha256 is checked against UPSTREAM.json; anything else is refused).

Mode 1, probe (default): the server is `command_server_probe`
(crates/blaze_server/src/bin/command_server_probe.rs), a GrpcServerImpl with a fake dispatcher
(commands echo, exit N, big N, sleep N, exec, crash). Each case runs fixed checks and prints
PASS or FAIL; this mode is a check list, not a comparison. Defaults: --probe
target/debug/command_server_probe (build it with
`scripts/cargo-capped build -p blaze_server --bin command_server_probe`), --scratch
~/.cache/blaze/work/server/s3/interop (wiped at the start of `run`; it must lie inside
~/.cache/blaze/work/server/). Run it under systemd-run, e.g.
  systemd-run --user --collect --wait --pipe -p MemoryMax=12G -p MemorySwapMax=0 \\
    scripts/server/interop-server run

Mode 2, --server-binary PATH: the server is a real server binary (Blaze: target/debug/blaze;
a private copy under ~/.cache/blaze/work/server/tools/bin runs, so a rebuild cannot change a
run). One sequence of real commands runs twice, each side on a fresh output user root and a
fresh copy of the interop workspace (scripts/server/common.py INTEROP_TEMPLATE, the same as
interop-client's), one after the other, at ~/.cache/blaze/work/server/s3/real:
  A  the expected side: the real client with its own real server (no --server_javabase);
  B  the real client with --server_javabase=<dir>, <dir>/bin/java -> the server binary.
  --control: B is A again (the real server; the noise floor).
Steps: version; info release workspace server_pid; build //:ok twice; query //...;
build //:fail; SIGINT to the client 10 s into build //:sleep (a 60 s genrule); info release;
shutdown; --max_idle_secs=5 info release, then the idle server must exit within 30 s; with
facts (same server across the commands, server alive after the cancel, gone after shutdown, new
server afterwards). Environment exactly HOME and PATH=/usr/bin:/bin; build, query and info get
--repository_cache=~/.cache/blaze/repo-cache --color=no --curses=no. Each step records the
client's true termination (`exit N` / `signal N NAME`, a timeout separately).
Normalisation: as interop-client (scripts/server/common.py Norm.RULES; the server_javabase
directory -> <SERVER_JAVABASE>). Per step (scripts/lib/compare_common.py classes through
common.judge_step): IDENTICAL (termination and transcript equal; the oracle side exited 0 or
the step declares its failure: REAL_DECLARED, `build //:fail` exit 1 and the cancelled build
exit 8), BOTH-FAILED (an undeclared equal failure: never a match), DIFFERENT (unified diff),
ORACLE-UNEXPECTED, SKIPPED (a step one side did not reach). An OOM kill in the unit during a
step (the unit has OOMPolicy=continue; its oom_kill counter is read around every step) makes
the step's termination `oom-kill`. The report starts with the client's and the server binary's
sha256, the checkout's commit and dirty state, and ends with compare_common's footer. The mode
runs itself in a `systemd-run --user --collect --wait --pipe -p MemoryMax=12G -p
MemorySwapMax=0 -p OOMPolicy=continue` unit under scripts/remote/unit-supervisor (status
recorded inside). Exit 0 only when every step is IDENTICAL, nothing timed out and no side
failed; 2 when the server binary is the oracle (outside --control) or --bazel is not the pin.
`selftest` drives this mode's command line with fake binaries.

Both modes always shut down, and failing that kill (SIGKILL), every server they started (pid
from <root>/*/server/server.pid.txt, only when alive and its command line names that root),
also when a case fails or the tool is interrupted.
"""

